brown baby a new book by the author  nikesh shukla  is addressed to a child his  five-year-old daughter  it discusses the world into which she is  a mixed-race girl is growing up  and the challenges she will face very  pleased to say uh nikesh  joins us on the programme this morning  great to have you on nikesh um  tell us a little bit about the book  brown baby it's described as a  as a love letter to to your daughter but  also  to your mother who i understand you lost  just just days after your first book  was published tell us about the book  yeah  so brown baby is a memoir and a  manifesto it's kind of  about me trying to explore how to raise  my  kids to be joyful and boundless but also  realistic about the world  in a world that feels quite bleak and  one that i feel very  sad and angry about it you know it's  basically about all of the stuff that  keeps me up at night as a parent you  know staring up at the  shadows on the ceiling thinking well how  do i  talk to my kid about racism how do i  talk to my kid about  the patriarchy and misogyny how do i  explain to her about  climate catastrophe and that you know  these are probably all things that  a lot of parents are thinking about and  um  while my parenting journey hasn't been  like particularly out of the ordinary or  spectacular i felt like  discussions around these things were  things that i was doing a lot so i  thought putting them into  the memoir would be a good way of um  sharing those anxieties with people who  are also going through them  but also in writing them i realized that  um  my there was just an absence uh my mom  as  as as you said she died quite suddenly  just before um  my my first novel came out and like 10  years ago it was very sudden it was it  was a real shock to our family and  just that absence i i felt it so much  more than i  ever thought i would do when my kids  were born because  she just wasn't there and so part of the  memoir became about  trying to um reconcile myself with my  mum's passing  um nickish i mean a lot of the problems  that you you can raise or the concerns  that you have you know  i have as well about my child but of  course i'm not viewing them through  a mixed race lens i i wonder how the  experiences of  of your gujarati mother who frankly  sounds an absolute character from what  i've read  uh how the experiences that that she  herself had and that you recount in the  book  how they will be of of note and of worth  to to  to others in a similar situation yeah  definitely but you know just to kind of  stress that  um you know our lenses may be different  but the fact that these are things that  we're both thinking about i think  you know bears bear some highlighting  you know the book is called brown baby  because it is about the specificity of  raising  um a mixed-race child who will you know  be  racialized as as brown or mixed-race for  for the rest of her life  um uh but also these are things as you  said we should all be thinking about  you know my mum was my mum was she was  the one who taught me about the value of  community like i'm  i'm a really strong believer in  community and um  doing community service you know it's  why i do so much  work around sort of diversity and  inclusion in publishing as well as doing  all the writing stuff  and a lot of that is because of you know  what she instilled in me is important  she  she always instilled in me this this  feeling that there was a journey  higher than my own there was a there was  a purpose greater than my own needs that  i needed to  give myself to and and without her i  probably  uh wouldn't have wouldn't have um  thought about things in that way  um that question we're about to run out  of time but just just a word from you  about you know how your children have  viewed recent events  of course black lives matter george  floyd i mean have you been able to kind  of broach those  topics with with two young people  yeah definitely so we we live in bristol 